<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Slay Idle Repeat — the SonarQube analysis settings.

  This file is the ONE place the analysis is configured. It is handed to the
  scanner with `/s:<absolute path to this file>` by
  build/ci/Invoke-SonarAnalysis.ps1, which is the only supported way to give the
  .NET scanner a settings FILE.

  🔴 THERE IS NO sonar-project.properties IN THIS REPOSITORY, AND ADDING ONE
  WOULD DO NOTHING. SonarScanner for .NET ignores sonar-project.properties
  entirely — it reads MSBuild's view of the solution instead. Somebody will
  eventually copy one in from a tutorial, watch the analysis run green, and
  conclude the exclusions below are active. They will not be. If you are here to
  add project-wide settings, add them HERE.

  ============================================================================
  🔴 NEVER PUT A COMMENT INSIDE A <Property> ELEMENT. EXPLANATIONS GO BETWEEN
  THEM. THIS IS NOT STYLE — IT SILENTLY TRUNCATES THE VALUE.

  MEASURED against scanner 11.2.1 on 2026-09-02, and then reproduced from a
  clean four-line file to be sure it was the comments and not something else.

  An earlier draft annotated each pattern inside a pretty multi-line list, which
  is valid XML and reads beautifully. The scanner deserialises a <Property> as a
  plain string, so mixed content does not concatenate the way
  XmlElement.InnerText would: ONLY THE TEXT AFTER THE LAST CHILD NODE SURVIVES.

  So this, in the draft that ran:

      <Property Name="sonar.coverage.exclusions">
        (an XML comment: "adapters are thin shells around vendor SDKs")
        src/adapters/**,
        ... six more entries ...
        (an XML comment: "test code is not production code")
        tests/**
      </Property>

  reached the analysis as exactly `tests/**`. The other five directories were
  gone, and SonarQube spent the run expecting coverage for sixteen uninstrumented
  assemblies. The engine log said so in one line nobody reads:

      INFO:   Excluded sources for coverage: tests/**

  Line breaks and indentation alone are harmless — the sibling property that had
  no comments in it came through complete, whitespace and all. It is the comment
  nodes that truncate. Values are kept on one line here anyway, because the rule
  "nothing but the value inside the element" is one somebody can actually follow.

  `begin` prints no warning about any of this and exits 0. So
  build/ci/Invoke-SonarAnalysis.ps1 rejects this file BEFORE it runs anything if
  a <Property> contains a comment or a line break, and re-reads the engine's own
  "Excluded sources" lines afterwards to confirm what actually reached it.

  ⚠️ And do not verify this by reading .sonarqube/conf/SonarQubeAnalysisConfig.xml
  — its <LocalSettings> holds only a SUBSET (sonar.exclusions is there;
  sonar.sourceEncoding and sonar.coverage.exclusions are not) and the rest still
  reach the engine by another route. It also shows sonar.exclusions with the
  coverage report path appended, which the scanner adds itself so the OpenCover
  XML is not analysed as source. Neither is a fault; both look like one.
  ============================================================================

  WHAT MAY NOT LIVE IN THIS FILE

  The scanner REFUSES a settings file containing sonar.projectKey,
  sonar.projectName, sonar.projectVersion or sonar.working.directory — they are
  per-invocation and belong on the command line (/k: /n: /v:).
  Invoke-SonarAnalysis.ps1 passes them. Likewise sonar.host.url and sonar.token:
  a URL and a credential are environment, not repository content, and committing
  either is how a token ends up in git history.

  ============================================================================
  THE COVERAGE COUPLING — READ BEFORE EDITING coverage.runsettings

  coverage.runsettings instruments SlayIdleRepeat.Core and
  SlayIdleRepeat.Application ONLY, by allow-list, at collection time. Every other
  assembly therefore produces no coverage data at all — not 0%, but *nothing*.
  Fed to SonarQube unqualified, that reads as "0% covered" across sixteen
  assemblies and drags the project's coverage figure to a number that measures
  the allow-list rather than the tests.

  sonar.coverage.exclusions below is the exact mirror of that allow-list: the
  files SonarQube must not expect coverage for, because nothing was ever
  instrumented to produce it. Those files are still ANALYSED — bugs, code smells
  and duplication are all still reported in them. Only the coverage metric skips
  them.

  Two files, one decision, so Invoke-SonarAnalysis.ps1 asserts they still agree
  before it runs: widen the allow-list in coverage.runsettings without widening
  this list and the run stops with an explanation rather than quietly reporting a
  coverage number nobody can interpret.
-->
<SonarQubeAnalysisProperties xmlns="http://www.sonarsource.com/msbuild/integration/2015/1">

  <!-- Directory.Build.props says nothing about encoding; the repository is UTF-8
       and .gitattributes enforces it. Stating it stops the scanner falling back
       to the platform default, which differs between a developer's Windows box
       and the Linux runner and silently changes how non-ASCII source is read. -->
  <Property Name="sonar.sourceEncoding">UTF-8</Property>

  <!-- Lets SonarQube attribute every issue to a commit and an author, and — more
       usefully — makes "new code" mean "changed since the reference point"
       rather than "appeared since the last analysis". Without it the whole
       clean-as-you-code model degrades to a whole-repository verdict.
       ⚠️ Needs the full history: a shallow clone makes blame data wrong. -->
  <Property Name="sonar.scm.provider">git</Property>

  <!-- ======================================================================
       ANALYSIS SCOPE

       An exclusion here removes a file from analysis COMPLETELY: no issues, no
       duplication, no metrics. Reserved for code nobody in this repository can
       change the shape of. Entry by entry:

         **/*.generated.cs, **/*.g.cs, **/*.Designer.cs
             Godot.SourceGenerators' dispatch switches — InvokeGodotClassMethod
             and friends, cyclomatic complexity up to 92, written by nobody and
             refactorable by nobody. The same files coverage.runsettings
             excludes by file, for the same reason.
         **/bin/**, **/obj/**
             Build output. Both also carry a copy of every generated file, so
             leaving them in doubles every finding in them.
         .godot/**
             Godot's import cache: regenerable, ~1260 MB, never committed.
         spikes/**
             Throwaway export spikes (O23). Not in SlayIdleRepeat.sln, so their
             C# is not analysed anyway — this covers the scripts and project
             files beside it, and says out loud that a spike is not held to the
             project's quality gate.
         artifacts/**, coverage/**, TestResults/**, .sonarqube/**
             Our own outputs. They exist on a developer's machine mid-run and
             would otherwise be analysed as if they were source.
       ====================================================================== -->
  <Property Name="sonar.exclusions">**/*.generated.cs,**/*.g.cs,**/*.Designer.cs,**/bin/**,**/obj/**,.godot/**,spikes/**,artifacts/**,coverage/**,TestResults/**,.sonarqube/**</Property>

  <!-- ======================================================================
       COVERAGE SCOPE — the mirror of coverage.runsettings' allow-list.
       See the 🔴 block at the top of this file before touching either.

         src/adapters/**
             Thin shells around vendor SDKs (23 keeps the domain free of them
             precisely so a hotspot in the domain is a hotspot in OUR logic).
             Not instrumented, so not measured.
         src/SlayIdleRepeat.Client/**
             Complexity dominated by generated dispatch.
         src/SlayIdleRepeat.Server/**, src/SlayIdleRepeat.Contracts/**
             The composition root and the wire contracts. Exercised end-to-end
             by the Server and Contract suites, not instrumented by
             coverage.runsettings.
         tests/**
             Test code is not production code. SonarQube's test-project
             detection already knows, but it is heuristic and this costs nothing.
       ====================================================================== -->
  <Property Name="sonar.coverage.exclusions">src/adapters/**,src/SlayIdleRepeat.Client/**,src/SlayIdleRepeat.Server/**,src/SlayIdleRepeat.Contracts/**,tests/**</Property>

  <!-- Duplication is a real signal in hand-written domain code and noise in
       generated dispatch. Test fixtures are deliberately repetitive — an arrange
       block that reads the same in twenty tests is a feature, and
       "de-duplicating" it is how a test suite becomes unreadable. -->
  <Property Name="sonar.cpd.exclusions">**/*.generated.cs,**/*.g.cs,tests/**</Property>

  <!-- ======================================================================
       COVERAGE AND TEST REPORT IMPORT

       Relative to sonar.projectBaseDir (the repository root). Wildcards are
       expanded by the scanner.

       🔴 OpenCover, not Cobertura — the exact inverse of the choice
       coverage.runsettings documents for the CRAP score, and for an equally
       specific reason: SonarQube's C# plugin has no Cobertura importer.
       sonar.cs.opencover.reportsPaths is the supported path for coverlet output,
       and pointing it at coverage.cobertura.xml produces a parse warning and 0%
       coverage — a green analysis with the one number missing.

       This is why coverage.runsettings emits BOTH formats. Its header says of
       OpenCover "Nothing reads it for CRAP"; this is the thing that reads it.
       Removing `opencover` from <Format> there breaks coverage here, and
       Invoke-SonarAnalysis.ps1 refuses to run if anyone does.
       ====================================================================== -->
  <Property Name="sonar.cs.opencover.reportsPaths">TestResults/**/coverage.opencover.xml</Property>

  <!-- The TRX files the run already writes. Gives SonarQube the pass/fail/skip
       counts and execution time per suite — the "Unit Tests" measures on the
       project page. Costs nothing; the files exist either way. -->
  <Property Name="sonar.cs.vstest.reportsPaths">TestResults/**/*.trx,artifacts/testresults/*.trx</Property>

</SonarQubeAnalysisProperties>
